    "result_text": "There's only one test class, and it passes. BUILD SUCCESS.\n\nThe change is complete. Here's a summary of what I implemented to reject creating an owner when the target city already holds 50 or more owners (HTTP 409):\n\n1. **`ClinicService` / `ClinicServiceImpl`** \u2014 Promoted the existing private `countOwnersInCity(String)` helper to a public interface method (`@Transactional(readOnly = true)`). It already existed for deriving per-city customer-code sequences, so I reused it rather than duplicating the counting logic, and fixed a misplaced Javadoc while I was there.\n\n2. **`OwnerCityCapacityExceededException`** \u2014 A new `RuntimeException` in the `rest.controller` package, mirroring the sibling `DuplicateOwnerTelephoneException` / `DuplicateOwnerHouseholdException` conflict types.\n\n3. **`ExceptionControllerAdvice`** \u2014 A dedicated `@ExceptionHandler` mapping the new exception to a **409 Conflict** `ProblemDetail` whose `errors` array names the `city` field, following the exact pattern of the existing 409 handlers.\n\n4. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 After the telephone-duplicate check and before persisting, it now rejects the request when `countOwnersInCity(city) >= MAX_OWNERS_PER_CITY` (a `static final long = 50` constant naming the rule).\n\nEach piece sits in its natural layer \u2014 counting in the service, the rule check in the create handler, the HTTP status mapping in the advice \u2014 with no logic duplicated. The full test suite (including the new `AcceptanceTest.coreRejectsWhenCityAtCapacity`) is green.",
